          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:40] Recall:</strong> What was the codename of the secret underground archive established by Emanuel Ringelblum to document daily life and Nazi atrocities in the Warsaw Ghetto?</li><li><strong>[03:50] Explain:</strong> Describe Abraham Lewin's diary entries recording the \"Great Deportation\" (<em>Grossaktion Warschau</em>) in the summer of 1942: how many Jews were loaded into cattle cars destined for Treblinka death camp each day?</li><li><strong>[06:30] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate the profound moral significance of the Oneg Shabbat archive: why did Ringelblum and his colleagues risk their lives to bury their writings in milk cans beneath the earth?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> <em>Oneg Shabbat</em> (\"Joy of the Sabbath\").</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Between 5,000 and 10,000 Jews were rounded up daily at the <em>Umschlagplatz</em> railway siding and packed into sealed freight cars with no water or sanitation. Lewin's diary captures the terror, weeping of mothers, and despair as over 265,000 Warsaw Jews were deported to Treblinka's gas chambers within seven weeks.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Ringelblum recognized that the Nazis intended not only to murder every Jewish person, but to erase their memory and rewrite history to depict their victims as subhuman vermin. Burying the testimonies, artwork, and statistics in sealed tin boxes and milk cans was an act of supreme spiritual resistance, ensuring that even if none of them survived, the undeniable truth of what happened would reach future generations.</li></ol>"
